    "body": "**Why.** The batch-invariant MoE unpermute sums the experts of each EP rank into one partial and adds the partials in rank order, so the EP size decides the summation tree. It read that size from the global parallel state, so a dispatcher with its own EP group on another grid was combined by the global grid's split. With EP=2 under a global EP of 1, all experts land in one partial, and the result no longer matches the rank-ordered combine this path mirrors.\n\n**What.**\n- `moe_utils.unpermute` and `batch_invariant.unpermute` take an optional `ep_size`.\n- `MoEAlltoAllTokenDispatcher.combine_postprocess` passes its own `ep_size`. It is the only dispatcher that reaches this path, because batch-invariant MoE training requires the AlltoAll dispatcher.\n- Callers that pass nothing keep the global EP size through a commented fallback.\n\n**Tests.** New `test_batch_invariant_unpermute.py` uses one token whose four expert outputs sum differently for EP=1, 2 and 4. It checks:\n- that `ep_size` decides the split, with every global accessor patched to fail;\n- the global fallback when nothing is passed;\n- the AlltoAll dispatcher on 2-rank EP groups under a global EP of 1, with the globals patched to fail.\n\nThe first and last cases fail on main. The dispatcher, batch-invariant RL and MoE determinism suites pass on 4 GPUs, as on main.\n\n**Compatibility.** `ep_size` is a new optional argument. There is no change on the standard grid, where the dispatcher's EP group is the global one. The summation code is unchanged, so the PR needs the `determinism-exempt` label.\n\nPart of #6307.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
